// Annex C.7, bullet 1: "No digital behavior or events are supported in
// Verilog-A." The construct under test is A.6.1 `continuous_assign ::= assign
// list_of_net_assignments ;`, which drives a net structurally, outside any
// analog block; the subset's only way to drive a signal is the `<+`
// contribution of §5.6 / A.6.10. (§5.5, cited here before, is "Accessing net
// and branch signals and attributes" — a neighbour that defines the probes, not
// the drive.)
//
// The one other `assign` fixture in the tree
// (ch07_mixed_signal/continuous_assign_unsupported.va) declares `wreal out;` in
// the same module and pins E0205, `wreal` and `assign` together, so no single
// rule is isolated. Here the target is a plain `wire` and the analog block is
// clean, so the diagnostic can only be about `assign`.
//
// The `wire w` carries no discipline, and that is not a second rule firing:
// C.4 bullet 3 reads "All Verilog-A modules shall have a discipline defined for
// each MODULE", and this module defines one (`electrical p`). 18 is the fixture
// for a module that defines none. An undisciplined digital net is also
// unavoidable here — `assign` drives nets, and any net given a discipline is
// either continuous (which no continuous assignment may drive, in AMS either)
// or discrete (which is C.4 bullet 1, fixture 07). A bare `wire` is the only
// target that isolates `assign`.
//
//! lrm C.7
//! lrm 5.6
//! lrm A.6.1
//! reject E0205
//! reject assign
module annex_c_continuous_assign_rejected(p);
    inout p;
    electrical p;
    wire w;
    assign w = 1'b1;

    analog I(p) <+ V(p);
endmodule
